blurb: The school board discussed updates on athletic field development, current enrollment
  trends, and the implementation of a new personal electronic device policy. They
  also reviewed funding for student homelessness prevention and reported on recent
  staff resignations.
votes: []
summary:
- topic: Athletic Field Updates
  text: The board received updates on both the high school athletic complex and the
    middle school field. The high school project is on time and budget for a May 2027
    groundbreaking, with design details being finalized. The middle school field is
    ready for fall soccer but will be limited to internal school use this season to
    allow the grass to fully establish.
- topic: Enrollment Trends
  text: Current enrollment shows a slight increase of 20 students overall compared
    to last year, though the general trend in Maine is declining. Kindergarten enrollment
    is being closely monitored, and an additional teacher and portable classroom are
    planned for Small School to manage class sizes.
- topic: Personal Electronic Device Policy Implementation
  text: A detailed plan was presented for implementing the new state-mandated 'bell-to-bell'
    ban on personal electronic devices. Implementation strategies vary by grade span,
    with high school students having devices collected and stored in lock boxes daily.
    The policy includes specific exceptions for IEPs, medical needs, and emergencies.
- topic: Student Homelessness Prevention Funding
  text: South Portland received a $40,000 award from the Maine Housing Student Homelessness
    Prevention program, making it one of seven districts to secure this competitive
    grant. These funds provide direct aid to unhoused students and families for critical
    needs like utilities, rent, and housing repairs, managed by the McKinney-Vento
    liaison.
- topic: Staff Resignations
  text: The superintendent reported several staff resignations, including an instructional
    support teacher and a humanities teacher. Additionally, the principals of Dyer
    Elementary and Skillin Elementary have resigned.
